			<item>
				<title>Mutex Counter</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/mutex/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/mutex/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;mutex-counter&#34;&gt;Mutex Counter&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#mutex-counter&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One hundred goroutines, one integer, each adding one. The answer should be&#xA;100. Run the check and watch it come back 97, 99, 100 — a different wrong&#xA;number every time. This exercise is about why one innocent line can&amp;rsquo;t be&#xA;trusted, and the one lock that makes it trustworthy.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;one-step-that-is-three&#34;&gt;One step that is three&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#one-step-that-is-three&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Reads as a single step. Compiles as three: load &lt;code&gt;c.n&lt;/code&gt; into a register, add&#xA;&lt;code&gt;n&lt;/code&gt;, store the result back. Two goroutines can interleave fatally — both&#xA;load 41, both add 1, both store 42 — and one increment vanishes without a&#xA;trace. No error, no panic, just a count that drifts downward under load.&#xA;Every shared-state bug in Go is some version of this interleaving.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Channel Pipeline</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/channels/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/channels/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;channel-pipeline&#34;&gt;Channel Pipeline&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#channel-pipeline&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The mutex lesson shared state and guarded it. This one inverts the&#xA;strategy: don&amp;rsquo;t share the slice at all. Each input travels to its own&#xA;goroutine, each result travels back over a channel, and the output is&#xA;assembled where exactly one goroutine — the caller — ever touches it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;sending-work-out-collecting-results-back&#34;&gt;Sending work out, collecting results back&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#sending-work-out-collecting-results-back&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: make the channel, send with &amp;lt;-, receive with &amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;ch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; make(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;chan&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// unbuffered: every send waits for a receiver&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;ch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;42&lt;/span&gt;             &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// send (blocks until received)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;v&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;ch&lt;/span&gt;            &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// receive (blocks until sent)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;channel&lt;/strong&gt; is a typed conduit between goroutines. &lt;code&gt;go&lt;/code&gt; launches the&#xA;worker; the channel carries the answer home:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Buffered Collect</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/buffered/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/buffered/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;buffered-collect&#34;&gt;Buffered Collect&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#buffered-collect&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The pipeline lesson&amp;rsquo;s channel was unbuffered: every send blocked until a&#xA;receiver arrived. Sender and collector moved in lockstep, each paced by the&#xA;other. Sometimes that&amp;rsquo;s exactly the backpressure you want. Here it isn&amp;rsquo;t —&#xA;&lt;code&gt;n&lt;/code&gt; workers shouldn&amp;rsquo;t queue behind one drain. This exercise gives the&#xA;channel a waiting room.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;unbuffered-is-a-handshake&#34;&gt;Unbuffered is a handshake&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#unbuffered-is-a-handshake&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;ch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; make(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;chan&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// capacity 0: send waits for receive, and vice versa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Every value changes hands directly, sender to receiver, in the same&#xA;instant. Elegant — and coupled: a fast worker idles until the collector&#xA;asks, a slow collector stalls every worker behind the current one. For&#xA;one-to-one rhythms that&amp;rsquo;s fine. For fan-out, it&amp;rsquo;s a traffic jam with extra&#xA;steps.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Select with Timeout</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/select/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/07_concurrency/select/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;select-with-timeout&#34;&gt;Select with Timeout&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#select-with-timeout&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Channels so far had one conversation each: send, receive, done. Real&#xA;programs juggle — data arriving &lt;em&gt;or&lt;/em&gt; a deadline expiring, whichever comes&#xA;first. Waiting on one channel while another fires is how fetches hang&#xA;forever. &lt;code&gt;select&lt;/code&gt; is how they don&amp;rsquo;t.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;waiting-on-whichever-answers-first&#34;&gt;Waiting on whichever answers first&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#waiting-on-whichever-answers-first&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: select runs the first ready case, blocking until one is&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;v&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;dataCh&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// data arrived first&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;After&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;200&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Millisecond&lt;/span&gt;):&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// deadline hit first&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;select&lt;/code&gt; blocks until &lt;strong&gt;one&lt;/strong&gt; of its cases can proceed, then runs exactly&#xA;that case. It reads like &lt;code&gt;switch&lt;/code&gt;, but the branches are channel operations&#xA;and the condition is &lt;em&gt;readiness&lt;/em&gt;, not equality. If several cases are ready&#xA;simultaneously, one is picked at random — fairness by design, so study the&#xA;implication: never write cases whose correctness depends on priority order.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
	</channel>
